        {
          "id": "q5",
          "type": "trueFalse",
          "prompt": "An ideal low-pass filter can be built exactly if enough components are used.",
          "answer": false,
          "explanation": "The impulse response of an ideal low-pass filter is a sinc function, which is nonzero for $t < 0$: the filter would have to respond **before** the impulse arrives. Such a non-causal system cannot be built. Real filters (Butterworth, Chebyshev, …) only approximate the brick-wall shape, with a transition band that gets narrower as the order increases."
        },
        {
          "id": "i4",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Distortionless transmission",
          "body": "A system does not distort a signal if the output is a scaled, delayed copy: $y(t) = Kx(t - t_0)$. Transforming gives the condition: constant gain $K$ and a phase that is **proportional to frequency**, so that every component is delayed by the same $t_0$.",
          "formula": "H(j\\omega) = K e^{-j\\omega t_0}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q6",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "Which frequency response is distortionless?",
          "options": [
            "$H(j\\omega) = 4e^{-j0.002\\omega}$",
            "$H(j\\omega) = \\frac{1}{1 + j\\omega/100}$",
            "$H(j\\omega) = 4e^{-j0.002\\omega^2}$",
            "$H(j\\omega) = 4 + j0.002\\omega$"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "$4e^{-j0.002\\omega}$ has constant magnitude 4 and linear phase $-0.002\\omega$: every component is amplified 4 times and delayed by 2 ms, so $y(t) = 4x(t - 0.002)$. The low-pass has a gain that varies with frequency, the $\\omega^2$ phase is not linear (different delays), and $4 + j0.002\\omega$ has both a varying gain and a non-linear phase."
        }
      ]
    },
